# Backtest data archives — LOCAL DATA, never repository content (2026-11-09).
#
# ⚠️ This rule used to be `nest/data/backtest-archive/*.gz`, which covered the archives but NOT
# `index.json` — so the generated index stayed TRACKED and churned on every export. And a
# `data/backtest-archive/` copy at the REPO ROOT was tracked too (10.4 MB, 4 archives, created
# 2026-07/08) while the service reads `process.cwd() + data/backtest-archive`, i.e. `nest/data/`.
# The root copy was STRANDED: nothing could read it, and the four archives it held were the only
# copies of those date ranges. They were moved into `nest/data/backtest-archive/` and indexed, so
# `GET /api/system/backtest-archive/import/:id` now serves them (verified: 10,000 rows returned).
#
# 282.7 MB lives there: a cache plus a generated index, neither of which a clone can use.
nest/data/

# Logs (generated at runtime)
nest/logs/

# ⚠️ Log FILES repo-wide. Two layers, deliberately:
#   1. File extensions — `*.log`, `*.log.err` (below) catch strays wherever they appear.
#   2. Directories — `nest/logs/` (above) and root `logs/` (bottom of this file) are ignored
#      wholesale: app telemetry lives under `nest/logs/`; the root is agent scratch.
#
# ⚠️ HISTORY, kept because it explains the traps (2026-09-16 → 2026-10-12):
#   • 56 log files (~7.5 MB) were once TRACKED and rewritten on every run — the working tree was
#     permanently dirty and `git status` stopped being a signal.
#   • 2026-09-16: this comment used to argue AGAINST ignoring `logs/` as a directory, because it
#     held `logs/scoring/*.jsonl` (live, served by the API), evidence screenshots and live.json.
#     That reasoning is SUPERSEDED — 2026-10-12: scoring MOVED under the app log root (shared
#     `resolveLogDir()` in log.service.ts; `scoring-log.service.ts` no longer follows `process.cwd()`),
#     and the root `logs/` became agent scratch (2026-10-10 rule at the bottom of this file).
#   • 2026-10-12: `git rm --cached` removed the last 33 tracked log files from the index. Nothing
#     under either logs root is versioned now — the package no longer carries runtime files.
*.log
*.log.err

# Python bytecode from the MT5 bridge and its canary — never tracked.
# `test_bridge_offset_guard.py` imports `mt5_bridge_v2.py` by path, which writes a
# __pycache__/ dir on every run.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Agent coordination cache (generated by scripts/agent-claim.cjs)
.github/issue-cache/snapshot.md

# Generated third-party attribution — NOT tracked, on purpose.
# scripts/generate-third-party-notices.cjs derives it from the installed dependency tree, so a
# tracked copy would be a second source of truth that silently drifts whenever a dependency moves.
# The publisher regenerates it into the release folder at package time, which is the only copy
# that matters. Run `npm run notices` when you want to read it locally.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt

# Free-edition release artefacts (scripts/publish-free-release.cjs) — the zip and its checksum are
# attached to the release rather than committed.
#
# ⚠️ `dist-free/` WAS TRACKED (18 files, 6.2 MB, including a source map) and those files were
# REMOVED on 2026-10-30, when the free edition was already gone and this directory was the last
# surviving copy of its build output. Measured 2026-09-25, and worth keeping: **an ignore rule is a
# declaration about files git has never seen — it says nothing about files git already has.** That
# block originally listed `dist-free/` (added in a8bf596) on the assumption a rebuild would then
# stop appearing, and a rebuild still produced 5 tracked-file changes. Untracking is a separate
# action from ignoring; this entry now covers a directory git no longer has.
dist-free/
trading-command-suite-free-*.zip
trading-command-suite-free-*.zip.sha256

# Compiled leftovers sitting beside seed SOURCE — nest/prisma/ (#897).
#
# ⚠️ These were TRACKED (23 files: 12 `.d.ts` + 11 `.js.map`) and are removed in the same change,
# so this block is what stops a stray `tsc` recommitting them. It does NOT untrack anything by
# itself — see the `dist-free/` note above: an ignore rule is a declaration about files git has
# never seen, and says nothing about files git already has.
#
# WHY THEY CANNOT BE BUILD OUTPUT: `nest/tsconfig.build.json` EXCLUDES `prisma`, and nothing under
# `nest/src` imports these modules — measured, not assumed. So a `.js`, `.d.ts` or `.js.map` here
# is never the executed file and never the compiled one; it is a copy that can silently disagree
# with the `.ts` next to it. #959's lesson: the plausible file is not always the load-bearing one.
nest/prisma/*.js
nest/prisma/*.js.map
nest/prisma/*.d.ts


# Bridge logs used to be written NEXT TO the bridge source and COMMITTED — measured
# 2026-10-10: 10 tracked files x 5 MB (two brokers x 5 rotations) = 50 MB of log churn
# synced with every commit. The bridge now writes to logs/mt5-bridge/ (gitignored); this
# rule stops a stray old-build process from recommitting anything here (#703).
scripts/mt5-bridge/*.log
scripts/mt5-bridge/*.log.*

# Root logs/ is agent + session WORKING SCRATCH — never repository material (owner direction, 2026-10-10).
#
# Measured that day: 576 files / 21.7 MB, of which 435 were TRACKED — issue-body drafts, close-comment
# drafts, canary scripts, build captures and probe outputs from many sessions, in five different naming
# styles. None of it is needed after the issue it serves is written; the durable record is the issue.
#
# New files under logs/ are therefore not tracked. Existing tracked files stay tracked until deliberately
# removed (this rule cannot untrack them) — see logs/README.md for the naming convention. Done 2026-10-12:
# the last 33 tracked files were removed from the index; the folder is fully unversioned.
logs/


# ── SESSION SCRATCH AT A PACKAGE ROOT — the class, not one instance (2026-11-07) ────────────────
#
# Owner, 2026-11-07: *"Look at nest/ … it contains a mix of completely different files … If that was
# another developer's project it wouldn't give me confidence in their tidiness."*
#
# Measured that day at nest/ (a Node package root), ALL of it TRACKED, in a PUBLIC repo:
#   5 × *.txt              session probe output; ti.txt is 10.8 KB of Prisma stack trace
#   1 × canary-<md5>.bak   151 KB backup of backtest.service.ts from a canary that never restored
#   3 × prisma.config.*    compiled .js / .js.map / .d.ts beside its own .ts source
#   13 × *.sql             one-off repairs sitting next to package.json
#   3 × check-* / test-insert.js / tmp-macd-test.ts / seed-*.cjs — with nest/scripts/ holding 135
#
# ⚠️ AND THE COMPILED-OUTPUT RULE ALREADY EXISTED — for nest/prisma/ only (#897, above). The exact
# same defect was sitting one directory up. That is why this block is by PATTERN and why the gate is
# scripts/validate-repo-hygiene.cjs: an ignore rule is a declaration about files git has never seen;
# the script is what catches a file that was already added with `git add -f`.

# Compiled output beside its own source. Never executed, never the build artefact — only a copy that
# can silently disagree with the .ts next to it.
nest/*.js
nest/*.js.map
nest/*.d.ts

# Scratch classes. A backup, an .old copy or a tmp- file is session material: it belongs in logs/.
*.bak
*.orig
*.old
*.rej
*.save
*.tmp
tmp-*
temp-*

# Session output saved as .txt. Real data files are named in .github/instructions/repository-layout.
nest/*.txt

# The bot fleet's numbers ARE the strategies. The repository is PUBLIC, so the generated
# fleet is built locally and never committed (#1258, register row 177).
nest/src/modules/trading/strategy-seed-generated.ts
nest/src/modules/trading/strategy-seed-generated.local.ts
